The growing demand for Earth observation (EO) to address environmental challenges has driven rapid expansion of its main carrier, Low Earth Orbit satellites, in recent years. Ground stations (GSs), the egress points of these networks, are increasingly congested by massive EO traffic, while their deployment is constrained by geographic, political, and budgetary factors. Although inter-satellite links (ISLs) can partially relieve this congestion by forwarding traffic to alternative GSs, existing ISL-based approaches can hardly resolve contention caused by biased GS distribution and may also raise sustainability concerns due to long ISL paths. In this paper, we propose OrbitTransit, a pickup-carry-offload (PCO) approach that leverages satellite mobility for data delivery and integrates ISLs for traffic diffusion to mitigate the resource contention inherent in PCO delivery. The proposed orbit-as-node framework and contention-avoidant delivery jointly determine the optimal hybrid PCO-ISL path to minimize energy consumption and balance GS traffic. Extensive experiments show that OrbitTransit reduces battery consumption by 47.16% and decreases task failures by 1.09× over state-of-the-art GS selection and routing algorithms.